# The level-three cusp-form dimension lower bound

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.cuspForm_finrank_lower_bound_level_three`
(`74b9a146-622e-4d74-af58-7aa82330d5cb`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `3b04d875-c31f-4715-8f6d-cb10497bfb3d`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

For every integer $k\ge3$, the cusp-form space of $\Gamma_1(3)$ satisfies

$$\left\lfloor\frac{k}{3}\right\rfloor-1\le\dim_{\mathbf C}S_k(\Gamma_1(3)).$$

This is the lower-bound consequence of the level-three dimension formula, valid in both parities. In
odd weight it combines with the numeric parabolic cohomology estimate to supply the remaining
level-three MTT dimension comparison.

## Explanation of the source proof

## Reduction to three low-weight forms

For $3\le k<6$, the required lower bound is zero. For $k\ge6$, take forms $A\in M_1(\Gamma_1(3))$,
$B\in M_3(\Gamma_1(3))$, and nonzero $D\in S_6(\Gamma_1(3))$ from the explicit seed theorem, with
q-orders zero and one for $A$ and $B$.

The proved weighted-monomial theorem gives

$$\dim
S_k(\Gamma_1(3))\ge\left\lfloor\frac{k-6}{3}\right\rfloor+1=\left\lfloor\frac{k}{3}\right\rfloor-1.$$

Finite-dimensionality follows from the already-Proved arithmetic modular-form theorem by injecting
cusp forms into modular forms. The strict period is one. The submitted Lean reduction compiles at
default resource limits and contains no sorry or extra axiom. The remaining analytic input is
exactly the seed theorem
[457b5c0a-b079-4e0f-a454-99c1bac6fa78](p2m:theorem/457b5c0a-b079-4e0f-a454-99c1bac6fa78), not an
assumed dimension formula.

For the planned seed construction, choose the normalized weight-one Eisenstein series for
discriminant $-3$, $B=\eta(3z)^9/\eta(z)^3$, and $D=\eta(z)^6\eta(3z)^6$. The eta exponent vectors
$(-3,9)$ and $(6,6)$ have the two divisibility sums $(24,0)$ and $(24,24)$; their cusp orders at
zero and infinity are $(0,1)$ and $(1,1)$. The sufficient modularity criterion and cusp-order
formula are Theorems 1.2 and 1.4 of [Allen et al.](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1901.10511), pp. 2–3. The
coprime-to-six corollary is not used. This seed construction remains an open Lean input; this
submission proves the reduction, not its analytic construction.

